## Part 2: Click to expand

We have a list. Now let's make it interactive.

Here's a simple goal. What if you could click any permit in the list and see everything we know about that shoot: the street where they're holding parking, the production category, the precinct, the country of origin. Click a close button, and it disappears.

This is a pattern that appears all over the web. You see a summary in a list, you click for the full record.

### Revisiting `$state`

You've used `$state()` before to track booleans like `isExpanded = $state(false)` and numbers like `billAmount = $state(0)`. This time we're going to store something different: an entire object, or nothing at all.

Add `$state(null)` to your script block in `+page.svelte`, right below the `$props()` line. Null is the word computer programmers use to refer to "nothing" or "no value." It's a common default for a variable that will later hold an object.

```svelte {emphasize-lines="5"}
<script>
  import ArticleHeader from '$lib/components/ArticleHeader.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
  let selected = $state(null);

  let headline = 'Recent Film Permits';
  let byline = 'NYCity News Service';
  let pubDate = '2026-03-16';
</script>
```

The `null` default value means "nothing is selected yet." When a user clicks a permit, we'll replace `null` with that permit's full object. When they close the card, we'll set it back to `null`.

### Wiring up a click handler

Add an `onclick` handler to the card `<div>` elements in your grid. When clicked, it should set `selected` to the current permit.

```svelte
<div class="permits">
  {#each data.permits as permit}
    <div class="card" onclick={() => selected = permit}>
      <h3>{permit.subcategoryname}</h3>
      <p>{permit.borough}</p>
    </div>
  {/each}
</div>
```

This is the same `onclick` pattern you used in Week 4 — an arrow function that updates a state variable. The only difference is that instead of flipping a boolean or toggling a number, we're storing the entire `permit` object.

Save the file. The cards won't look any different yet, because nothing is reading from `selected`. Let's fix that.

### Rendering the detail card

Add a conditional block below the grid that renders when something is selected:

```svelte
<div class="permits">
  {#each data.permits as permit}
    <div class="card" onclick={() => selected = permit}>
      <h3>{permit.subcategoryname}</h3>
      <p>{permit.borough}</p>
    </div>
  {/each}
</div>

{#if selected}
  <div class="detail-card">
    <button onclick={() => selected = null}>Close</button>
    <h2>{selected.subcategoryname}</h2>
    <p><strong>Category:</strong> {selected.category}</p>
    <p><strong>Location:</strong> {selected.parkingheld}</p>
    <p><strong>Borough:</strong> {selected.borough}</p>
    <p><strong>Dates:</strong> {selected.startdatetime} – {selected.enddatetime}</p>
  </div>
{/if}
```

Save and try it. Click any card in the grid. A detail panel should appear below with a summary of that record. Click "Close" and it disappears.

SELECTED PERMIT SCREENSHOT

## Part 3: Styling with CSS variables

We have a working interactive page. Now let's make it look like something.

### Styling the cards

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. Add a `<style lang="scss">` block at the bottom of the file. We'll start with the card grid.

```svelte
<style lang="scss">
  @use '$lib/styles' as *;

  .permits {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--spacing-sm);
  }

  .card {
    padding: var(--spacing-sm);
    border: var(--border-width-thin) solid var(--color-border);
    cursor: pointer;

    &:hover {
      background-color: var(--color-light-gray);
    }
  }
</style>
```

The `.permits` rule uses [CSS Grid](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_grid_layout) to arrange the cards. The `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr))` expression tells the browser to create as many columns as will fit, each at least 200 pixels wide. On a wide screen you'll see four or five columns; on a narrow screen, fewer. The layout adapts automatically.

Notice the `&:hover` inside the `.card` block. That's [SCSS](https://sass-lang.com/documentation/style-rules/parent-selector/) syntax — the `&` character refers to the parent selector, so `&:hover` compiles to `.card:hover`. It lets you keep related styles grouped together instead of writing a separate rule. This is the reason we use `<style lang="scss">` instead of plain `<style>` — it gives us this nesting shorthand.

Save and check your browser. The permits should now appear as a grid of small cards with a hover state.

### Highlighting the selected card

When you click a card, the detail panel appears, but nothing in the grid tells you which permit you're looking at. Let's fix that by highlighting the selected card.

Svelte has a shorthand for conditionally applying a class called the [class directive](https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/class). Update your card `<div>` to use it:

```svelte
<div class="card" class:active={selected === permit} onclick={() => selected = permit}>
```

The `class:active={selected === permit}` attribute adds the `active` class to the element whenever the expression on the right is true — that is, whenever this particular permit is the currently selected one.

Now add the style for it. Add this rule to your `<style lang="scss">` block:

```scss
  .card.active {
    background-color: var(--color-light-gray);
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
  }
```

Save and try clicking a few cards. The selected card should get an accent-colored border while the detail panel shows below.

### Styling the detail card

Now let's style the card itself. Add these rules to your `<style lang="scss">` block:

```scss
  .detail-card {
    margin-top: var(--spacing-lg);
    padding: var(--spacing-md);
    background-color: var(--color-light-gray);
  }

  .detail-card button {
    float: right;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
```

Save and check your browser. The detail card should now look clean and organized.

FINAL SCREENSHOT

Notice that every value here comes from the design system variables we set up in Week 6. No hardcoded colors, no hardcoded sizes. If you wanted to use this same page inside a publication with a different accent color, you'd only need to change `--color-accent` in one place.

## Homework

### Task 1: Extract a FilmPermit component

Right now all of the detail card markup lives directly in `+page.svelte`. In a real project you'd want to pull it out into its own component — both to keep the page file clean and to make the card reusable.

Create a new file at `src/lib/components/FilmPermit.svelte`. Move the detail card markup and its styles out of `+page.svelte` and into this new component. The component should accept the permit to display and a function to call when the user clicks Close as props.

Once it's working, import it in `+page.svelte` and replace the `{#if selected}` block with the component. Your page styles should now only cover the cards.

Send me the link to your working GitHub Pages URL.

### Task 2: Fetch your own dataset

Remember the dataset you pitched from the [NYC Open Data portal](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/) in your earlier homework? Now it's time to start building it.

Create a new repository from our template. Write a `fetch-data.js` script that downloads the dataset you pitched and saves it to `src/lib/data/` as a JSON file. Use the same pattern we followed in today's lesson — `fetch` the Socrata API endpoint, filter or limit the results with query parameters, and write the output with `fs.writeFileSync`.

Your script should run cleanly with `node fetch-data.js` and produce a readable JSON file. Send me the link to your GitHub repository.
